INT. GREENHOUSE WEDDING TENT - NIGHT

Fairy lights. Orchids. A cake leaning slightly. The band has stopped. Guests have drifted outside.

NAOMI ADEYEMI-BRAUN, thirties, in a champagne gown, stands at the cake table with a knife and no cake cut. Across from her, SEBASTIAN KOWALCZYK, thirties, bow tie hanging loose, holds two flutes and gives neither of them up.

Beside them, a bridesmaid, PRIYA SOLANKI, twenties, freezes mid-reach for a canapé. Behind, the officiant, FATHER LEOPOLD GAUDET, sixty, dozes upright on a folding chair.

Naomi sets the knife down. Sebastian sets a glass down. They both pick up their own again. She drinks. He doesn't.

NAOMI
You were always going to leave.

SEBASTIAN
I'm standing right here.

PRIYA
(low)
Guys.

Naomi and Sebastian turn their heads together, then back. Priya retreats a step, canapé in hand. Father Gaudet wakes, blinks, smiles at the ceiling, and is out again.

NAOMI
I meant tomorrow. Next month. Whenever it gets hard.

SEBASTIAN
It's already hard.

She takes off her ring, looks at it, puts it on the cake table. He steps forward, picks it up, and slides it back onto her finger. She lets him. Neither says anything.

PRIYA
Should I cut the cake?

NAOMI
No.

SEBASTIAN
Yes.

They look at each other. A small laugh escapes Naomi. Sebastian's mouth twitches. Priya picks up the knife and cuts, a crooked first slice, and hands the plate to Father Gaudet, who wakes and takes it.
